---
/** A random deck generator and the walkthrough of how it is built. The
 * code the page runs is the code the page shows: one JavaScript file,
 * split at its section comments so each step sits beside its prose. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeExample from "../../components/CodeExample.astro";
import source from "../../fun/random-deck.js?raw";
import { ATLAS, SPELLBOOK } from "../../fun/random-deck.js";

const sections = source.split(/^(?=\/\/ ── )/m).map((chunk) => chunk.trim());
const step = (n: number) => sections.find((s) => s.startsWith(`// ── ${n}.`)) ?? "";
const sourceUrl = "https://github.com/sadkinglabs/kairos-archive/blob/main/src/fun/random-deck.js";
const elements = ["Air", "Earth", "Fire", "Water"];
const lines = source.split("\n").length;
---
<Base title="Random deck" canonical="/fun/random-deck" description="Pick elements and a spell split, get a random Sorcery deck list drawn to the copy rules from the Kairos Archive query API. Plain JavaScript, on the page, step by step.">
  <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/fun">Fun Zone</a></p>
  <h1>Random deck</h1>
  <p class="intro">Pick the elements and how the spellbook splits, and deal: one avatar, sixty spells, thirty sites, no card above what its rarity allows. Every part of the deck is one search query, the same syntax as the search box, answered by the <a href="/docs/query">query API</a>. The list shows the query each part came from. It is {lines} lines of plain JavaScript, and all of it is on this page.</p>

  <form id="deck-form" class="deck-form">
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Elements</legend>
      <div class="chips">{elements.map((e) => <label><input type="checkbox" name="element" value={e} checked={e === "Fire" || e === "Water"} /> {e}</label>)}</div>
      <p class="muted small">Cards with no element are always in. A card with two elements needs both.</p>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Spellbook, 60 spells</legend>
      <div class="split">{Object.entries(SPELLBOOK).map(([type, n]) => <label>{type}s <input type="number" name={type} value={n} min="0" max="60" /></label>)}</div>
      <p class="muted small">The atlas is {ATLAS} sites. A split that does not add up to 60 gives a spellbook of that size, which the list will say.</p>
    </fieldset>
    <button type="submit">Deal a deck</button>
  </form>
  <pre id="deck" class="rules deck" aria-live="polite">Your deck appears here.</pre>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>
  <p>JavaScript, the plain kind: no framework, no build step, no key. Seven parts, in the order they run; the code under each heading is read from the file this page is running.</p>

  <h3>0. The rules</h3>
  <p>Four copies of an Ordinary, three of an Exceptional, two of an Elite, one of a Unique. Thirty sites. Sixty spells, split by type, and the default split. Two avatars a random deck should not hand you.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(0)} lang="javascript" title="the rules" />

  <h3>1. One search query per card type</h3>
  <p>The deck is nothing but search results. <code>t:minion (e:fire,water or e:none)</code> is every minion that has Fire or Water or no element. Paste it into the search box and you see the same cards the deal draws from. The avatar query drops two names with <code>-!"Name"</code>.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(1)} lang="javascript" title="the query" />

  <h3>2. Every card the query matches</h3>
  <p>The API answers two hundred cards a page and says whether there are more, so the loop asks until there are not. The answer is remembered: a second deal with the same choices asks nothing. The API allows sixty requests a minute from one address, and a deal is about eight.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(2)} lang="javascript" title="fetching" />

  <h3>3. Only the chosen elements</h3>
  <p><code>e:fire,water</code> also admits a Fire and Air card, because it has Fire. One line keeps a card only when every element it has was chosen.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(3)} lang="javascript" title="the filter" />

  <h3>4. Draw to the copy rules</h3>
  <p>Shuffle, then walk the pool taking a random number of copies of each card, never more than its rarity allows, until the part is full. A pool too small for its part gives a short part, which the list says, never a fifth copy.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(4)} lang="javascript" title="the draw" />

  <h3>5. A deck</h3>
  <p>One query per part, one draw per part: the avatar, each spell type at its size, the sites.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(5)} lang="javascript" title="the deal" />

  <h3>6. The list</h3>
  <p>Each part with its count and the query it came from, then its cards by name. Text, so it pastes anywhere.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(6)} lang="javascript" title="the list" />

  <h3>7. The form</h3>
  <p>Read the boxes, deal, print. This is the only part that needs a browser.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(7)} lang="javascript" title="the form" />

  <h2>Make it yours</h2>
  <p>Change the queries. <code>s:alpha</code> for one set, <code>cost&lt;=3</code> for a cheap deck, <code>-is:errata</code> to skip cards whose text has changed, <code>k:spellcaster</code> for a theme: anything the <a href="/syntax">search syntax</a> can say, the deck can be drawn from. Parts 0 to 6 run in Node as they are; send a <code>User-Agent</code> that names your tool, as the <a href="/usage">usage terms</a> ask.</p>

  <h2>All of it</h2>
  <p><a href={sourceUrl}>random-deck.js on GitHub</a>.</p>
  <CodeExample code={source} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js" />
</Base>

<script>
  import "../../fun/random-deck.js";
</script>

<style>
  .deck-form { border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--card); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.2rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .deck-form fieldset { border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .deck-form legend { font-weight: 600; }
  .deck-form .chips { margin: .5rem 0; font-size: 1rem; }
  .deck-form .split { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .75rem 1.25rem; margin: .5rem 0; }
  .deck-form .split label { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; }
  .deck-form .split input { width: 5rem; min-height: 38px; }
  .deck-form .small { font-size: .85rem; margin: .25rem 0 0; }
  .deck { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: .85rem; line-height: 1.5; }
</style>
